---
import Base from "@layouts/Base.astro";
import { siteConfig } from "~/settings";

const features = [
  {
    title: "Many packs, one repository",
    body: "Host every mod pack your community uses in one place. Packs can depend on each other, so a base pack with core mods can be extended by event-specific packs.",
  },
  {
    title: "Delta patching",
    body: "Only the parts of a PBO that changed between versions are downloaded. Saves bandwidth for both server and players.",
  },
  {
    title: "Speed above all",
    body: "Indexes are cached on the server and the client, so files are not re-scanned on every sync. Indexing and downloads run on multiple threads.",
  },
  {
    title: "Packs are cheap",
    body: "A pack is just a couple of files. Easy to create and delete, which makes one-off event packs painless.",
  },
  {
    title: "Optional addons",
    body: "Packs can ship optional addons that players toggle on or off. You can also launch a pack with all optionals disabled.",
  },
  {
    title: "External addons",
    body: "Players can add addons from their own disk to a pack and toggle or remove them later, without touching the server.",
  },
  {
    title: "Local packs",
    body: "Packs that only exist on your machine, for testing or private mod lists.",
  },
  {
    title: "Server deployment",
    body: "Deploy a pack to a dedicated server. pamm links the addons and their signing keys, builds the -mod launch parameter, fills in your script templates, and runs your post-deploy commands.",
  },
  {
    title: "Mission maker mode",
    body: "Extra tools for mission makers in the GUI: launch without optionals, manage local packs, and open the repo on disk.",
  },
  {
    title: "Windows and Linux",
    body: "Runs natively on Windows. On Linux it starts Arma through Steam and Proton, with native and Flatpak Steam both supported.",
  },
  {
    title: "GUI and CLI",
    body: "A desktop app for players, and a scriptable command-line tool with a man page for server admins and automation.",
  },
  {
    title: "Automatic migrations",
    body: "When the repo layout changes, server and client repos are migrated automatically the next time they are opened.",
  },
];
---

<Base>
  <section class="hero">
    <img src="/logo.png" alt="The pamm logo" width="160" height="160" />
    <hgroup>
      <h1>pamm</h1>
      <p>Personal ARMA Mod Manager</p>
    </hgroup>
    <p>
      A lightweight, fast Arma 3 mod manager for communities that host their own
      mods.
    </p>
    <p class="actions">
      <a
        href={`${siteConfig.repoUrl}/releases/latest`}
        role="button"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Download</a
      >
      <a
        href={siteConfig.repoUrl}
        role="button"
        class="secondary outline"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">View on GitHub</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="features">
    <h2>Features</h2>
    <div class="features">
      {
        features.map((f) => (
          <article>
            <h3>{f.title}</h3>
            <p>{f.body}</p>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p>
      <small
        >pamm is not compatible with other mod managers. Planned features are
        tracked in the <a
          href={`${siteConfig.repoUrl}/issues`}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">issues</a
        >.</small
      >
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="packs">
    <h2>Repositories and packs</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>repository</strong> is what your community hosts: one URL, with
      its own name, description, and optional icon, banner and colour. It holds
      any number of <strong>packs</strong>. A pack is a named list of addons that
      players sync and launch together, with its own description, icon and launch
      parameters.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each pack can have one <strong>parent</strong>. A pack inherits all of its
      parent's addons, and the parent inherits from its own parent, so you can
      build chains as deep as you need:
    </p>
    <pre><code>My Community              repository
├── core                  CBA, ACE, TFAR, ...
│   ├── campaign          core + campaign terrains and assets
│   │   └── campaign-ww2  core + campaign + WW2 mods
│   └── training          core + training maps
└── modern-event          standalone pack, no parent</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Sync</strong> downloads the whole chain, starting at the root. A
        parent's addons live in the parent pack, so they're downloaded once no matter
        how many child packs build on it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Launch</strong> loads the pack's own addons plus the required addons
        of every ancestor. Launch parameters are not inherited; each pack keeps its
        own.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Optional addons</strong> from every pack in the chain show up when
        you launch a child pack. Your choice is saved on the pack that owns the addon,
        so enabling an optional in <code>core</code> enables it for every pack built
        on <code>core</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Deploy</strong> resolves the same chain on the server, so a dedicated
        server loads the same required addons as players. Optional addons are left
        out.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Local packs</strong> can use any pack as their parent, including a
        pack from the server or another local pack. A local pack can't be removed
        while another local pack depends on it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      On disk, every pack is its own folder next to <code>repo.config.json</code>,
      with a <code>pack.config.json</code> and an <code>addons/</code> directory.
      Adding a parent is a single field in that config, which is what makes small
      event packs on top of a shared base so cheap.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="get-started">
    <h2>Get started</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <article>
        <header><strong>For players</strong></header>
        <p>
          Install the desktop app from the <a
            href={`${siteConfig.repoUrl}/releases/latest`}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">latest release</a
          > and add your community's repository URL. Or use the CLI:
        </p>
        <pre><code>pamm_cli init-remote &lt;url&gt;  # join a repo
pamm_cli sync &lt;pack&gt;          # download changes
pamm_cli launch &lt;pack&gt;        # start Arma</code></pre>
      </article>
      <article>
        <header><strong>For server admins</strong></header>
        <p>
          A repo is split into the source you edit and a <code>www/</code> build
          output that any HTTP server can serve.
        </p>
        <pre><code>pamm_cli init       # create an empty repo
pamm_cli add-pack   # register a pack
# drop addons into &lt;pack&gt;/addons/@name/
pamm_cli build      # populate www/</code></pre>
        <p>
          <small
            >Point your web server at <code>&lt;repo&gt;/www/</code>. Any server
            that supports multiple range requests works; pamm is tested with
            Caddy.</small
          >
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="linux">
    <h2>Linux</h2>
    <p>
      On Linux, pamm launches the Windows version of Arma 3 through Steam and
      Proton. Arma can live in any of your Steam libraries.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Guided Steam setup.</strong> Run <code>pamm_cli linux</code> or click <em
          >Steam setup</em
        > in the GUI to see exactly which launch options or Flatpak
        overrides your mod location needs. pamm never changes Steam's configuration
        itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>TeamSpeak in the Proton prefix.</strong> Install and launch the Windows
        TeamSpeak 3 client in the same prefix as the game.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Prefix tools.</strong> Open a file browser inside the prefix, or stop
        everything running in it, including the game.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Follows your Proton version.</strong> Switch Arma to another Proton
        version in Steam and pamm picks it up.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    text-align: center;
    padding-block: var(--pico-block-spacing-vertical);
  }
  .actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--pico-spacing);
  }
  .hero hgroup {
    margin-block: 1rem;
  }
  .features {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--pico-grid-column-gap);
  }
  .features article {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .features h3 {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }
  .features p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  section {
    scroll-margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  #features > p {
    margin-top: var(--pico-spacing);
  }
</style>
